WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.550
- [Narrator] This is the eastern bongo.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:06.620
These beautiful chestnut
and white-striped creatures

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:10.717
can only be found in four
isolated regions of Kenya.

00:00:10.717 --> 00:00:13.634
(mysterious music)

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.370
Eastern bongos are fairly large in size

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:21.810
and can weigh anywhere
from 500 to 900 pounds,

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.810
and their horns can be
as long as 40 inches.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.660
In fact, their horns are as wide

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.543
as the widest part of their body.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.000
Despite their large size,

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.780
eastern bongos are rather timid creatures.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:34.890
They are herbivores.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.760
They tend to eat and are most active

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:38.380
during twilight and dusk

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.103
as the darkness helps them
to hide from predators.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.880
There is estimated to be about
100 bongos left in the wild,

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:49.690
putting them well below the
critically endangered threshold.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.480
Their biggest threats are hunting

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.373
and the destruction of their habitat.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.240
In an effort to protect the eastern bongo,

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.650
various parts of their
native habitat in Kenya

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.800
have been designated as shelters for them,

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.130
allowing the eastern bongo to
move about freely and safely

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:05.823
without fear of poachers.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.473
This is the eastern bongo.

00:01:15.016 --> 00:01:17.599
(bright music)

